{"node_id": "real_073_Adaptation-in-a-heterogeneous-environment-II_7_EG_001", "node_type": "evidence", "status": "SHADOW_CANDIDATE", "unit_type": "TEXT", "source_atom_ids": ["D0001"], "source_fragment_ids": ["D0001_F001"], "source_visual_bundle_ids": [], "plain_text": "Furthermore, it turns out that, when persistence occurs, that is, $\\lambda_H <0$ , the nature of\nthe stationary states deeply depends on the number of hosts. Namely, when $H = 1,\\,2$ , the\nstationary states are proportional to the principal eigenfunctions (and the study of their\n“ shapes ” has recently received a lot of attention, see e.g. [2, 9, 18, 19] ). This is no longer\ntrue in general in the case $H>2$ , due to a possible symmetry breaking. More precisely,\nwe have the following results.", "semantic_text": "Furthermore, it turns out that, when persistence occurs, that is, $\\lambda_H <0$ , the nature of the stationary states deeply depends on the number of hosts. Namely, when $H = 1,\\,2$ , the stationary states are proportional to the principal eigenfunctions (and the study of their “ shapes ” has recently received a lot of attention, see e.g. [2, 9, 18, 19] ). This is no longer true in general in the case $H>2$ , due to a possible symmetry breaking. More precisely, we have the following results.", "visual_description": null, "geometry_members": [{"member_id": "D0001_geometry", "bbox": [156, 162, 973, 317], "bbox_source": "dolphin_v2_resized_image_pixels", "source_atom_id": "D0001"}], "native_visual_components": [], "production_modified": false, "visual_description_status": "UNVERIFIED_RETRIEVAL_HINT", "node_policy": "bounded-v2", "scoring_mode": "embedding_geometry"}
